# Copy to .env before running `docker compose up`.
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Nothing here is a secret today: the service stores nothing, so there is no
# datastore to protect and no key to hold. Every value below tunes inference or
# says which build to serve.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The model
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There are two ways the backend can get its weights, and only one of them is
# on by default.
#
#   1. Baked in from MODEL_IMAGE, at build time. This is the default, and the
#      only thing that works on a host that builds from a Git clone - the
#      weights are ~420MB and gitignored, so a clone does not carry them.
#   2. Mounted from MODEL_DIR, at run time. Opt-in, for local work on a
#      retrained model. It shadows (1).
#
# MODEL_IMAGE is the image holding nothing but the weights, built by
# scripts/build_model_image.sh and consumed as a build stage by
# backend/Dockerfile.
#
# THE REGISTRY HOLDING IT MUST BE PRIVATE, and so must any backend image built
# from it - backend/Dockerfile copies /models out of the model image, so it
# holds the same bytes. The model is DrBERT fine-tuned on the DEFT 2021 corpus
# of French clinical cases. The corpus is not distributable, which settles it
# on its own; on top of that, the possibility that fine-tuned weights carry
# memorised spans of the clinical text they were trained on cannot be ruled
# out. A public package here is a data-protection incident, not a licence
# question, and cannot be taken back from whoever already pulled it.
#
# On GHCR a package LINKED to a repository takes that repository's visibility,
# and linking happens automatically when CI pushes with GITHUB_TOKEN or when
# the image carries an org.opencontainers.image.source label. A manually
# pushed, unlinked user-scoped package is created private - but "probably
# private" is not a basis for publishing these weights, so
# scripts/build_model_image.sh has you create the package with a weightless
# placeholder, confirm it reads private, and only then push. It deliberately
# has no push flag.
#
# The default below names that private package. If this machine has no
# credential for it, build the weights locally instead and point this at the
# result - no registry involved:
#
#   scripts/build_model_image.sh          # -> med-assist-model:local
#   MODEL_IMAGE=med-assist-model:local
#
# A build with neither a credential nor a local tag fails on the pull, loudly
# and before anything starts. That is deliberate.
MODEL_IMAGE=ghcr.io/jonperron/med-assist-model:1.0

# Where the NER weights are on this machine, for option (2). It has no effect
# unless docker-compose.dev.yml is in play, which is what carries the mount:
#
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.dev.yml up
#
# or uncomment COMPOSE_FILE below and use plain `docker compose up`. With the
# mount, a retrained model is a `docker compose restart backend` with no
# rebuild and no re-publish; without it, retraining means a new MODEL_IMAGE.
#
# The directory holds the files `from_pretrained` reads - config.json,
# model.safetensors, tokenizer.json, tokenizer_config.json.
#
# Write it as an absolute path, or as a relative one starting with `./`. Compose
# reads a bare `models` as the name of a Docker volume rather than as a
# directory, and it does not expand `~` - `~/models` creates a directory
# literally named `~` beside this file. A relative path resolves against the
# directory holding docker-compose.yml, not against your shell's.
#
# Every one of those mistakes fails the same quiet way: a path that does not
# exist is created empty by Docker rather than refused, and an empty directory
# shadows the baked-in weights just as well as a full one - so the backend
# starts, fails the load, and answers 503 on /readyz and on the analysis
# routes. The interface reports that as the service being unavailable and says
# no more, because it says nothing about configuration to a clinician. If that
# warning is on screen, check whether the dev overlay is in play at all; then
# check this path, then `docker compose logs backend`.
#
# Point it only at a directory holding the weights, owned by whoever runs the
# deployment: whatever it names is mounted into the backend's filesystem.
#
# Commented out because the overlay requires it rather than defaulting it: a
# default is how you mount an empty directory over good weights by forgetting a
# variable. Uncomment both lines below together, or neither.
# MODEL_DIR=./backend/models

# Uncomment to make the mount above the default for `docker compose` in this
# checkout. Compose reads COMPOSE_FILE from this file before anything else.
# Leave it commented on any host that deploys: this file is gitignored, so a
# clone never carries it, and that is what keeps a deployment on the baked-in
# weights.
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.dev.yml

# Threads torch may use for one inference. Left unset, docker-compose.yml
# defaults it to BACKEND_CPU_LIMIT below, because torch reads the host's core
# count rather than the container's CPU quota and would otherwise start a
# thread per host core inside a two-core allowance. Set it to 0 to get torch's
# own default back, or lower to leave the host responsive.
NER_INFERENCE_THREADS=2

# How many documents may be inside the model at once. One by default: a second
# set of activations is what puts a small machine into swap. Above one, the same
# model object is called from several threads, which transformers does not
# document as safe - prefer running more backend processes. If you do raise it,
# pin NER_INFERENCE_THREADS at the same time, or each inference claims every
# core and they contend.
NER_MAX_CONCURRENT_INFERENCES=1

# Set to `development` only to expose the mock endpoints. Never in a deployment.
APP_ENV=production

# Which browser origins may call the API, comma-separated, each written as
# scheme://host[:port] with no trailing slash and no port the scheme already
# implies - both are dropped rather than refused, since a browser omits them.
# This is the other half of NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL below: that one tells the
# browser where the API is, this one tells the API which page is allowed to ask.
# It is not authentication: a client that is not a browser ignores CORS
# entirely, and nothing else here authenticates one. Serve the interface from
# anywhere other than the default and both have to move, or the browser drops
# every answer. Left unset it stays on the local frontend origin rather than
# widening - the API answers with credentials, and a browser refuses a
# credentialed response allowed to `*` anyway, so the wildcard is rejected at
# startup instead of quietly breaking every call.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000

# What each container may take from the host. The backend holds the model
# resident, so its limit is the one that matters. Measured: 470 MiB idle,
# 504 MiB peak across a batch of five long documents; it starts and analyses at
# 512m with nothing to spare. The default is roughly four times the peak,
# because a large PDF is expanded in memory by its parser. The RAM-backed /tmp
# that uploads spool into is charged against this limit too.
BACKEND_MEMORY_LIMIT=2g

# Inference is CPU-bound and this is the setting that decides how long a batch
# takes. Keep NER_INFERENCE_THREADS in step with it: measured on a 14-core host
# under a 2-core quota, five long documents took 11s with the two matched and
# 215s with torch left to pick its own thread count.
BACKEND_CPU_LIMIT=2

FRONTEND_MEMORY_LIMIT=512m
FRONTEND_CPU_LIMIT=1

# The URL the browser uses to reach the backend. Baked into the frontend bundle
# at build time, so changing it requires a rebuild of the frontend image.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000
